# Deployment

The whole application runs from one Docker Compose stack. A server needs Docker
and nothing else — no Node, no pnpm, no Prisma CLI, no build step by hand.

## Prerequisites

- Docker Engine with the Compose plugin
- DNS A records for `trano.be`, `www.trano.be` and `api.trano.be` pointing at the
  server, before the first start: certificates are issued over HTTP, so the
  names must already resolve here
- Ports 80 and 443 reachable from the internet

## Environment

    cp .env.example .env

Then fill it in. Every variable is marked `[PRODUCTION]`, `[OPTIONAL]` or
`[DEVELOPMENT]` in that file. The ones a deployment cannot start without:

| Variable | What it is |
|---|---|
| `POSTGRES_USER`, `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `POSTGRES_DB` | the database, created on first start |
| `APP_HOST`, `APP_HOST_WWW`, `API_HOST` | the hostnames the proxy answers on |
| `ACME_EMAIL` | where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings |
| `APP_BASE_URL` | `https://trano.be` — Auth0 builds its callback and logout URLs from it |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | `https://api.trano.be` — where the browser calls the API |
| `AUTH0_DOMAIN`, `AUTH0_AUDIENCE` | verified by the backend on every request |
| `AUTH0_CLIENT_ID`, `AUTH0_CLIENT_SECRET`, `AUTH0_SECRET` | the frontend's login flow |

`.env` is never committed. It holds the database password, the Auth0 secret and
the mailbox password.

`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` is a **build-time** value: Next inlines it into the
JavaScript the browser downloads. Changing it needs
`docker compose build frontend`, not just a restart.

## Build and start

    docker compose build          # first time, or after pulling changes
    docker compose up -d

That single command starts everything in order: PostgreSQL, then the migrations,
then the API once they succeeded, then the web application, then the proxy.

## Everyday commands

    docker compose ps             # what is running, and whether it is healthy
    docker compose logs -f        # follow everything
    docker compose logs -f backend
    docker compose restart backend
    docker compose down           # stop everything; DATA IS KEPT
    docker compose up -d          # start it again

## Migrations

Applied automatically on every `up`, by a one-shot `migrate` container that runs
`prisma migrate deploy` and exits. The API waits for it to succeed, so no
request is ever served against an unmigrated schema.

`migrate deploy` only applies migrations that have not run yet. It never resets,
never drops and never seeds. The development seeds are not part of this stack:
a deployment starts from the data it already has.

## Volumes

| Volume | Holds | Lost if deleted |
|---|---|---|
| `traxo-postgres-data` | the entire database | every Trip, price and setting |
| `traxo-pdf-storage` | uploaded and emailed transport orders | the original PDFs |
| `traxo-letsencrypt` | issued certificates | re-issued on next start, subject to Let's Encrypt rate limits |

They survive `docker compose down`, image rebuilds and container replacement.

## Backups

Both volumes matter, and a database dump alone is not a backup — the PDFs live
outside it:

    docker compose exec postgres pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB" > backup.sql
    docker run --rm -v traxo-pdf-storage:/data -v "$PWD:/backup" alpine \
      tar czf /backup/pdf-storage.tar.gz -C /data .

## Destructive commands

    docker compose down -v

**Deletes the volumes**: the database, every stored transport order and the
certificates. There is no undo and no confirmation prompt.

    docker volume rm traxo-postgres-data

The same, for one volume.

Neither is ever run by the stack itself. Nothing in this repository resets a
database automatically.

## Trying the production stack locally

The deployment stack publishes only the proxy, on 80 and 443, and the proxy
routes by hostname and asks Let's Encrypt for certificates. None of that works
on a laptop, so there is an override that leaves the proxy out and publishes the
applications directly:

    docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml up -d

Then open <http://localhost:3100>. The API is on <http://localhost:3000>.

Same images, same migrations, same volumes as a server — only the proxy is
absent, so TLS and the hostname routing are not exercised.

Two things to know:

- **The ports clash with the development servers.** `pnpm dev:frontend` and this
  stack both want 3100. Stop one before starting the other, or set
  `LOCAL_APP_PORT` / `LOCAL_API_PORT` in `.env`.
- **It has its own, empty database** (`traxo-postgres-data`). The development
  data lives in `tms-postgres-data` and is not shared. That separation is
  deliberate.

Stop it again with:

    docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.local.yml down

## Local development

Development still runs from source, against its own database:

    pnpm db:up          # docker-compose.dev.yml — PostgreSQL and pgadmin
    pnpm dev:backend
    pnpm dev:frontend

The two stacks share no volumes and no project name, so neither can stop or
delete anything belonging to the other.

